<Project>

  <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Declaring Debug-VM and Release-VM to every project
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    EVERY PROJECT HAS TO DECLARE THE NAMES, NOT JUST BEHAVE CORRECTLY UNDER THEM.

    `dotnet build <solution> -c Debug-VM` is content to hand a project a configuration the
    project never mentioned, so the command line never notices this is missing. Visual Studio
    is not: it resolves a solution build type onto a project configuration OF THE SAME NAME and
    checks the project has one. Opening the solution and choosing Debug-VM therefore reports

      Invalid project mappings. See log file

    against every project whose $(Configurations) is still the SDK default `Debug;Release`.

    eng/Broiler.Configurations.props is what makes the names BEHAVE (decompose to a base
    configuration, pick the engine). This file is what makes them EXIST. They are separate
    problems and, as below, they cannot run at the same time.

    ── Why this is a .targets and not part of the .props ─────────────────

    A project's own <Configurations> is an unconditional assignment in the csproj body, which
    MSBuild evaluates AFTER Directory.Build.props, so every value the props side could have set
    is simply overwritten for any project that spells its own list out. Directory.Build.targets
    is evaluated after the csproj body, so this is the only point that sees what a project
    actually ended up declaring.

    ── Why the import is unconditional ──────────────────────────────────

    The mapping in Broiler.Configurations.props is imported from two places under a sentinel, so
    that a project reached from the props side does not get it a second time from the targets
    side. That is right for a decomposition, which must happen once, and wrong for this, which
    must happen last. The append below is idempotent — it checks before adding — so it needs no
    sentinel, and having none is what lets it run for the chaining projects too.

    ── What a regression looks like ─────────────────────────────────────

    Silence, and then a developer opening the solution in Visual Studio. Nothing on the command
    line reports it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Configurations Condition="'$(Configurations)' == ''">Debug;Release</Configurations>

    <!--
      Delimited on both ends so that a substring test cannot match a longer name: without the
      semicolons `Debug-VM` is a substring of a hypothetical `Debug-VM2`, and the append would be
      skipped for a project that never declared it.

      The test is a property function in a property VALUE rather than in a Condition on purpose.
      MSBuild's condition parser does not read nested quotes reliably (MSB4092), and this file
      exists because a configuration problem went unnoticed; it should not introduce another one.
    -->
    <BroilerConfigurationsProbe>;$(Configurations);</BroilerConfigurationsProbe>
    <BroilerDeclaresDebugVm>$(BroilerConfigurationsProbe.Contains(';Debug-VM;'))</BroilerDeclaresDebugVm>
    <BroilerDeclaresReleaseVm>$(BroilerConfigurationsProbe.Contains(';Release-VM;'))</BroilerDeclaresReleaseVm>

    <Configurations Condition="'$(BroilerDeclaresDebugVm)' != 'true'">$(Configurations);Debug-VM</Configurations>
    <Configurations Condition="'$(BroilerDeclaresReleaseVm)' != 'true'">$(Configurations);Release-VM</Configurations>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
